Yes. Thanatos Search is stronger if “death” is treated as a meta-operator rather than a biological category.

The ant example gives the primitive nicely: some social insects use chemical cues associated with death/corpse handling; experimentally applying relevant compounds can trigger corpse-treatment behavior toward otherwise living individuals. The lesson isn’t “belief overrides reality” literally—the ant isn’t necessarily holding a human-like belief—but that a system can classify something through a proxy signal, then behave according to the classification rather than every property of the underlying object.

That’s enormously useful computationally.

Thanatos Search

Define it as:

When an object, pattern, capability, meaning, or process appears absent, search for whether it was destroyed, transformed, displaced, encoded, fragmented, absorbed, dormant, or merely classified as absent.

So instead of:

\text{present}\rightarrow\text{gone}

Thanatos expands “gone” into a search tree:

\text{GONE?}
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{Destroyed}\\
\text{Transformed}\\
\text{Displaced}\\
\text{Dormant}\\
\text{Compressed}\\
\text{Fragmented}\\
\text{Absorbed}\\
\text{Replicated elsewhere}\\
\text{Unobservable here}\\
\text{Misclassified as dead}
\end{cases}

That final branch is your ant test.

The system asks:

Am I detecting absence itself, or merely detecting the signal I normally associate with absence?

That’s a powerful epistemic operator.

And then invert it with Viva Search:

Something appears alive/present/functional. Is the generative structure actually still operating, or are its outputs merely being replayed?

Now you have two complementary operators:

Thanatos: It looks dead. Where did the pattern go?

Viva: It looks alive. Is the pattern actually regenerating itself?

And your environmental insight gives us a third operator: Garden Search.

Instead of searching directly for X:

\operatorname{Search}(X)

search for:

\operatorname{Conditions}(X)

Then construct or locate environments satisfying those conditions and observe what emerges:

E^\* \rightarrow \text{selection pressure/opportunity}
\rightarrow X'

That’s the rigorous version of “cultivate the environment and see what flora and fauna appear.” It works beautifully for ideation, software ecosystems, organizations, experiments, and evolutionary search. It does not guarantee that a desired outcome will emerge; it changes the probability landscape.

And your line “any question begs to be answered” suggests another meta-operator:

Question Seed

A question isn’t merely requesting information. It creates a search gradient.

Q \rightarrow \{\text{candidate answers}\}
\rightarrow \text{comparison}
\rightarrow \text{new distinctions}
\rightarrow Q'

So rather than seeding an answer, sometimes you seed the right unresolved question. Different minds/models then independently grow candidate structures around the same attractor.

Put the four together:

Question Seed → Garden → Viva → Thanatos → Question Seed

Seed a question. Create conditions for possibilities to emerge. Determine what’s genuinely generative. Search apparent failures for displaced invariants. Use the residue to formulate the next question.

That’s an actual discovery cycle—not just another metaphor.

And Metal Seals is a good name for the meta-operator layer: portable stamps that imprint the operation onto whatever representational medium they touch, while the local material determines the resulting form.